From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Name the limit that stops thread creation.

With 0866 in, threads work -- eighteen complete init_thread in a run,
reach BTCpuThreadInit, enter BTCpuSimulate and run guest code, winmm
starts its devices thread, waveOutOpen returns and sound plays: the alive
tick reads audio=1027 where it has read audio=0 since this port began.
Then creation starts failing, at about the eighteenth thread:

    wine-ps5: spawn_thread tid=0068 status=c0000017 stack=1027e70000+1048576

c0000017 is STATUS_NO_MEMORY, but spawn_thread maps every pthread_create
failure onto it, so the number that tells them apart never leaves the
function: EAGAIN is a resource ceiling, ENOMEM a heap, EINVAL the stack
handed to it. On this console those point in very different directions --
libc malloc here was measured past a gigabyte without refusing, and the
one refusal on record came back errno 22, EINVAL, not ENOMEM -- so this is
worth measuring rather than guessing at.

Keep the return value and the live thread count, and log only on failure:
a working spawn costs nothing, and there are a great many of them now.

⚠ The other half of this, the NULL thread_data that faulted the exit path
on the way down, went into 0882 instead -- that patch introduces the
in-process server thread which is the thread with no Wine data, and it
owns pthread_exit_wrapper in this series.
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c
index f52e727195..54d575ac81 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c
@@ -1376,6 +1376,7 @@ static NTSTATUS spawn_thread( struct thread_data *data )
     pthread_t pthread_id;
     pthread_attr_t attr;
     NTSTATUS status = STATUS_SUCCESS;
+    int spawn_errno = 0;
 
     pthread_sigmask( SIG_BLOCK, &server_block_set, &sigset );
     pthread_attr_init( &attr );
@@ -1384,7 +1385,7 @@ static NTSTATUS spawn_thread( struct thread_data *data )
     pthread_attr_setscope( &attr, PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM ); /* force creating a kernel thread */
     InterlockedIncrement( &nb_threads );
     data->ps5_spawn_us = ps5_slow_log() ? ps5_now_us() : 0;
-    if (pthread_create( &pthread_id, &attr, (void * (*)(void *))server_init_thread, data ))
+    if ((spawn_errno = pthread_create( &pthread_id, &attr, (void * (*)(void *))server_init_thread, data )))
     {
         InterlockedDecrement( &nb_threads );
         status = STATUS_NO_MEMORY;
@@ -1397,8 +1398,14 @@ static NTSTATUS spawn_thread( struct thread_data *data )
      * thread is wedged somewhere between pthread_create and wow64's per-thread
      * hook, and nothing in that stretch says a word. These four lines are that
      * stretch; stderr from ntdll reaches the session log as "WINE <text>". */
-    ERR( "wine-ps5: spawn_thread tid=%04x status=%08x stack=%p+%zu\n",
-             (unsigned)data->tid, (unsigned)status, get_kernel_stack( data ),
+    /* ⚠ pthread_create's own return is the only thing that names the limit --
+     * EAGAIN a resource ceiling, ENOMEM a heap, EINVAL the stack handed to it
+     * -- and the caller throws it away, turning every one of them into
+     * STATUS_NO_MEMORY. Keep it, with the live thread count. Logged only on
+     * failure: a working spawn costs nothing, and there are many now. */
+    if (status)
+        ERR( "wine-ps5: spawn_thread tid=%04x FAILED pthread_create=%d threads=%d stack=%p+%zu\n",
+             (unsigned)data->tid, spawn_errno, (int)nb_threads, get_kernel_stack( data ),
              (size_t)kernel_stack_size );
 #endif
     pthread_attr_destroy( &attr );
